From: Vorapol Rinsatitnon <vorapol.r@pm.me>
Date: Fri, 04 Sep 2026 20:04:27 +1200
Subject: [PATCH] path/filepath: skip the VHD tests where the Hyper-V cmdlets are missing

createMountPartition (CL 565136, Go 1.23) creates the volume that two
EvalSymlinks tests need, using New-VHD and Mount-VHD. Those cmdlets come from
the Hyper-V PowerShell module, which first shipped with Windows 8.

	no Hyper-V module  ->  the script fails
	                   ->  the test continues without a volume

Check for one of the cmdlets before running the script, with an explicit exit
code that PowerShell 2 does report, and skip on that. This also skips before
the cleanup is registered, so nothing tries to dismount a volume that was never
mounted. Where the module is present nothing changes.


diff --git a/src/path/filepath/path_windows_test.go b/src/path/filepath/path_windows_test.go
index d60903f6..763ee8f7 100644
--- a/src/path/filepath/path_windows_test.go
+++ b/src/path/filepath/path_windows_test.go
@@ -484,6 +484,17 @@ func TestWalkDirectorySymlink(t *testing.T) {
 
 func createMountPartition(t *testing.T, vhd string, args string) []byte {
 	testenv.MustHaveExecPath(t, "powershell")
+
+	// The script below needs the Hyper-V cmdlets, which are not always
+	// installed. Ask for one of them rather than waiting for the script to
+	// fail, because PowerShell 2, which is what Windows 7 ships, exits
+	// zero when a script fails and the failure below would go unnoticed.
+	probe := testenv.Command(t, "powershell", "-Command",
+		"if (-not (Get-Command New-VHD -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue)) { exit 1 }")
+	if out, err := probe.CombinedOutput(); err != nil {
+		t.Skip("skipping test because Hyper-V is not available: ", err, string(out))
+	}
+
 	t.Cleanup(func() {
 		cmd := testenv.Command(t, "powershell", "-Command", fmt.Sprintf("Dismount-VHD %q", vhd))
 		out, err := cmd.CombinedOutput()
